<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-5-3-modular-arithmetic" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Modular Arithmetic</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      In the last section, we remaked that, for a fixed integer <m>d</m>, the function <m>f: \mathbb{Z} \to \{0, 1, \ldots, d\}</m> such that <m>f(n) = n \text{ mod } d</m> generates a <em>partition</em> of the integers. We make this idea formal here.
    </p>
  
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-residue-classes">
    <title>Residue Classes Modulo <m>m</m></title>

    <p>
      We'll illustrate this using an example. Consider the function <m>f : \mathbb{Z} \to \{0, 1, 2\}</m> given by <m>f(n) = n \text{ mod } 3</m>. Then, following <xref ref="thm-div-alg"/> closely, notice that:
    </p>

    <p>
      <md number="no">
        <mrow> f^{-1}(0) \amp = \{ \ldots, -6, -3, 0, 3, 6, \ldots \}, </mrow>
        <mrow> f^{-1}(1) \amp = \{ \ldots, -5, -2, 1, 4, 7, \ldots \}, </mrow>
        <mrow> f^{-1}(2) \amp = \{ \ldots, -4, -1, 2, 5, 8, \ldots \}. </mrow>
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      We see that <m>f^{-1}(0) \cup f^{-1}(1) \cup f^{-1}(2) = \mathbb{Z}</m> and that <m>f^{-1}(k) \cap f^{-1}(j) = \varnothing</m> for any <m>k \neq j</m>. As we saw in Homework 4, this implies that division by 3 <em>partitions</em> the integers into nonoverlapping sets. We call these sets <em>residue classes modulo 3</em>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Since all of the numbers in a given residue class <q>behave</q> the same way under <m>f</m>, we might consider each of these numbers to be <q>equivalent</q> in some sense. For example, since <m>1 \text{ mod } 3 = 7 \text{ mod } 3</m>, <m>1</m> and <m>7</m> are the same up to division by <m>3</m>. We capture this equivalence with the following definition.
    </p>

    <definition xml:id="def-congruence">
      <statement>
        <p>
          If <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> are integers and <m>m</m> is a positive integer, then <em><m>a</m> is congruent to <m>b</m> modulo <m>m</m></em> if <m>m \mid a - b</m>. If <m>a</m> is congruent to <m>b</m> modulo <m>m</m>, we write <m>a \equiv b \; (\text{mod } m)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <example xml:id="ex-congruence">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Determine whether
      </p>

      <p>
        <ol>
          <li><p><m>22</m> is congruent to <m>7</m> modulo <m>5</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p><m>24</m> is congruent to <m>14</m> module <m>6</m>.</p></li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li><p>Since <m>5</m> divides <m>22 - 7 = 15</m>, we have <m>22 \equiv 7 \, (\text{mod } 5)</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Since <m>6</m> does not divide <m>24 - 14 = 10</m>, we have <m>24 \not\equiv 14 \, (\text{mod } 5)</m>.</p></li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <p>
    <xref ref="def-congruence"/> tells us how to map between the world of congruences and the world of equations. Being able quickly code switch between the two is an important skill in number theory.
  </p>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-congruence-equation">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> be integers and <m>m</m> be a positive integer. Then <m>a</m> is congruent to <m>b</m> modulo <m>m</m> if any only if there exists an integer <m>k</m> such that <m>a = b + km</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <proof>
      <p></p>
      <p>
        <m>(\implies)</m> Suppose <m>a \equiv b \, (\text{mod }m)</m>. Then <m>m \mid a - b</m>, and so there exists an integer <m>k</m> such that <m>a - b = km</m>. Therefore, <m>a = b + km</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>(\impliedby)</m> Suppose <m>a = b + km</m> for some integer <m>k</m>. Then equivalently <m>a - b = km</m>, and hence <m>m \mid a - b</m>. Hence, <m>a \equiv b \, (\text{mod }m)</m>.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </theorem>
  
  <p>
    More coming soon...
  </p>
  
  </subsection>
  
